WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:08.216
Stop Saying, from BBC Learning English dot com.   

00:00:13.383 --> 00:00:17.532
Hi, I'm Sugah and I come from Japan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.532 --> 00:00:28.410
When someone asks me how I am, I usually say 'I'm good', but is this wrong? Should I stop saying it?

00:00:38.897 --> 00:00:48.520
Hi, Tim here! You're right: people often use 'good'&nbsp;
in this way, with a verb. Normally we think of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:54.929
'good' as an adjective and 'well' as an adverb,&nbsp;
so you'd expect to hear things like:

00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:00.062
Hi Tim, how are you? 

00:01:00.062 --> 00:01:02.062
I'm well, thanks.

00:01:04.061 --> 00:01:07.061
So why do we so&nbsp;often hear things like:

00:01:07.061 --> 00:01:09.061
How are you?

00:01:09.061 --> 00:01:11.344
I'm good. Thanks, Tim.

00:01:11.344 --> 00:01:18.490
It's because with some verbs, which&nbsp;
we sometimes call 'link verbs', it's okay to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:30.940
'good' in this way. These include 'be', 'seem',&nbsp;
'appear', 'look', 'taste', 'smell', 'sound', and 'feel'.

00:01:38.525 --> 00:01:40.325
He smells good.

00:01:43.341 --> 00:01:46.207
He looks good.

00:01:50.207 --> 00:01:56.206
But I hear it in other places&nbsp;too. For example, in the movie, I heard, "He cooks&nbsp;real good".

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:04.490
Ah, well, this is an example of language&nbsp;
changing. In some varieties of English, for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:14.171
US English, adjectives are occasionally used when&nbsp;
we would use an adverb in British English. Compare these:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.171 --> 00:02:17.938
He sings pretty bad. &nbsp;

00:02:17.938 --> 00:02:21.937
He sings pretty&nbsp;badly. 

00:02:23.937 --> 00:02:25.937
That's real good. 

00:02:25.937 --> 00:02:34.103
That's really good. But that accent is really bad.

00:02:36.336 --> 00:02:42.052
Thanks Tim, that&nbsp;explanation was real good, but you sing pretty&nbsp;bad.

00:02:42.052 --> 00:02:44.352
How do you know that? 

00:02:46.002 --> 00:02:55.351
Yeah, on second thoughts, maybe I shouldn't sing. Best keep the day job. Bye!

